    "https://www.higheredjobs.com/institution/details.cfm?JobCode=179610446&Title=Student%20Success%20Specialist": {
      "title": "Student Success Specialist",
      "description": "Student Success Specialist - HigherEdJobs Skip to main content Sign Up | Log In Log In Sign Up Job Search By Category Administrative Faculty Executive By Location Metro/Region State/Province Country Online/Remote By School Four-Year Institutions Two-Year Institutions All Institutions Institutional Profiles By Type Dual Career Today's Jobs Adjunct/Part-Time Inclusive Workplace Military-Friendly Advanced Search My Account Signed out Account Home Job Alerts Resume/CV Other Documents My Jobs Organizer Profile Account Settings News & Resources News Advice & News Higher Ed Careers Author in Residence Ask the Expert Newsletters HigherEdJobs Podcast Resources Career Resources Job Search Tips Resume/CV Writing Service Workplace Inclusion Search Firms Employers Home Login Create Account Job Postings Post a Single Job Job Packs Unlimited Postings VIP Positions Automated Postings Other Products Digital Advertising Resume Database Institutional Profile Interim Search View All Products Posting Upgrades Inclusive Workplace Upgrade Inclusive Workplace Spotlight Priority Job Upgrade Military Upgrade Social Media Upgrade Featured Executive Position More Info Our Audience Testimonials Pay Your Invoice Contact Us Home Jobs Institution Job Listings Job Detail Student Success Specialist Centenary University in Hackettstown, NJ Apply Now Type: Full-Time Salary: $43,000 - $50,000 per year Posted: 3 days ago Application Due: 11/24/2026 Category: Academic Advising ; +1 Disability and Accessibility Services Centenary University Mission & Priorities Centenary launches students into successful careers and lives. Centenary advances three strategic priorities: Student Success, University Vitality, and World Engagement. Position Summary The Student Success Specialist serves as a primary advocate, coach, and case manager for students who will benefit from additional support to persist, achieve academic success, maintain enrollment, and graduate. Student Success Specialists maintain an assigned caseload of students identified through early alerts, academic performance indicators, faculty referrals, self-referrals, accessibility needs, transition challenges, or other risk factors affecting student success. Through personalized intervention, coaching, coordination of services, and ongoing monitoring, Student Success Specialists help students develop the skills, behaviors, and support networks necessary to thrive academically, socially, and personally. Specialists work collaboratively with faculty, advisors, athletics, residence life, accessibility services, counseling resources, financial aid, student affairs, and families, when appropriate, to remove barriers to student success. Core Responsibilities and Expected Outcomes 1. Maintain an Assigned Caseload of Students Requiring Additional Support Responsibilities Maintain  regular contact with assigned students using individualized success plans. Assess  academic, personal, social, behavioral, financial, and engagement factors  affecting student performance and persistence. Develop  personalized intervention strategies to address identified barriers. Meet  regularly with students to establish goals, monitor progress, and promote  accountability. Teach  and reinforce skills including: Time  management Organization How  to learn Study  strategies Executive  functioning Goal  setting Self-advocacy Communication Problem-solving Connect  students to appropriate campus resources and support services. Maintain  accurate case notes, action plans, and student records. Outcomes Students  demonstrate measurable improvement in academic performance, engagement,  and utilization of support services. Assigned  students persist semester-to-semester at rates exceeding historical  risk-group averages. Students  demonstrate increased self-management, resilience, and independence. Students  remain actively connected to academic and campus support systems. 2. Lead Early Intervention and Retention Efforts Responsibilities Monitor  academic alerts, attendance concerns, behavioral concerns, and other risk  indicators. Utilize  data systems and retention platforms to identify students requiring  intervention. Conduct  outreach within established service standards when students are flagged. Coordinate  intervention plans with faculty, advisors, coaches, and campus partners. Track  intervention effectiveness and adjust support strategies as needed. Participate  in student concern and retention case review meetings. Outcomes Students  receive timely intervention before academic or enrollment problems become  severe. Increased  percentage of students successfully resolving academic or engagement  concerns. Improved  first-to-second year retention and term-to-term persistence rates. Reduction  in withdrawals, stop-outs, and academic dismissals among identified  students. 3. Coordinate Comprehensive Student Support Services Responsibilities Serve  as the central point of coordination for assigned students. Facilitate  referrals and follow-up with: Academic  support services Faculty Accessibility  Services Career  Development Financial  Aid Residence  Life Counseling  and Wellness resources Student  Engagement programs Advocate  for students while promoting personal responsibility and self-advocacy. Assist  students in navigating university policies, procedures, and support  resources. Support  students during transitions, including first-year adjustment, major  changes, probation recovery, return from leave, and graduation planning. Outcomes Students  effectively access and utilize available university resources. Reduction  in service gaps and unresolved student concerns. Increased  student confidence in navigating university systems and requirements. Students  make timely progress toward degree completion. 4. Support Student Success Programming and Skill Development Responsibilities Develop  and facilitate workshops, seminars, and group programs focused on student  success. Deliver  programming related to: Academic  achievement Executive  functioning Personal  wellness Career  readiness Growth  mindset Financial  literacy Resilience Belonging  and engagement Create  educational materials and resources that support student success  initiatives. Collaborate  with faculty and campus offices to integrate student success strategies  into the student experience. Outcomes Increased  participation in student success programming. Students  demonstrate improved academic and life management skills. Greater  student engagement and campus connection. Positive  student feedback regarding support services and programming. 5. Utilize Data, Assessment, and Continuous Improvement Practices Responsibilities Monitor  caseload metrics and student outcomes. Document  all student interactions and interventions in institutional systems. Track  student success indicators including: GPA Credit  completion Retention Graduation  progression Resource  utilization Prepare  reports and recommendations for leadership. Participate  in assessment and continuous improvement initiatives. Identify  trends and emerging student needs. Outcomes Accurate  and timely student records and intervention documentation. Data-informed  decision-making regarding student success initiatives. Improved  efficiency and effectiveness of student support programs. Demonstrated  contribution to university retention and graduation goals. 6. Serve as a Member of the Student Success Team Responsibilities Participate  actively in Student Success meetings, committees, and strategic  initiatives. Collaborate  with faculty and staff to promote a campus-wide culture of student  success. Assist  with student onboarding, orientation, transition, and persistence  initiatives. Participate  in professional development related to student success, advising,  coaching, mental health awareness, accessibility, retention, and higher  education best practices. Support  university events, outreach activities, and special projects as assigned. Outcomes Strong  cross-functional collaboration across campus. Consistent  implementation of student success practices. Positive  relationships with faculty, staff, students, and families. Ongoing  enhancement of student support services and outcomes. Core Performance Outcomes for the Position At the highest level, Student Success Specialists will be accountable for: Student Persistence Assigned  students re-enroll at rates meeting or exceeding university targets. Increased  first-to-second year retention for students receiving services. Academic Achievement Improvement  in semester GPA and successful course completion rates among supported  students. Reduction  in academic probation and dismissal rates. Student Engagement Increased  utilization of support services and campus resources. Increased  participation in campus activities and engagement opportunities. Degree Progression Students  remain on track toward graduation. Improved  credit completion and progression milestones. Student Satisfaction Students  report feeling supported, connected, and equipped to succeed. Positive  satisfaction ratings from students receiving services. Documentation and Intervention Standards Timely  outreach to identified students. Accurate  case management documentation. Consistent  follow-up and closure of student concerns. Qualifications ·  Bachelor’s degree in Social Work, Education, Psychology, Counseling, or a related field. ·  Minimum of 1-3 years of experience working with individuals in an educational setting preferred; equivalent experience considered. ·  Knowledge of disability services, accommodation processes, and applicable accessibility laws and regulations a benefit. ·  Strong interpersonal, organizational, oral, and written communication skills. ·  Ability to manage multiple responsibilities and maintain accurate documentation. ·  Proficiency with Microsoft Office Suite, adaptive technology, database systems, and scheduling platforms. Demonstrated commitment to student success, equity, and inclusive practices. Centenary University is a learning community comprised of multifaceted and complex differences and we welcome all. We offer a supportive environment that promotes acceptance, a culture of service, skills of critical thought, and purposeful action. We are responsible for the efforts needed to create the change we seek. Equal Employment Opportunity has been, and will continue to be, a fundamental principle at Centenary University where employment is based upon personal capabilities and qualifications without discrimination because of race, color, religion, sex, age, national or ethnic origin, sexual orientation, marital status, service in Armed Forces, disability or any other protected characteristic as established by law. This policy of Equal Employment Opportunity applies to policies and procedures relating to recruitment, hiring, promotion, compensation, benefits, termination and other terms and conditions of employment. Apply Now Related Searches: Academic Advising Disability and Accessibility Services Want to learn more about working for this employer? View Profile Advertisement Create your free job search account Edit and manage Job Alerts Post your resume/CV Track your applications Join Now Have an account? Sign in To Apply for this Job Email: sasha.jackson@centenaryuniversity.edu OR Apply through HigherEdJobs Please be sure to read the job posting carefully and submit all required information. Job Seekers Search Jobs Job Seeker Login Advice & News Newsletter Sign-Up Employers Post A Job All Recruitment Products Employer Login Testimonials Company About Us Contact Us Advertise with Us Our Audience Job Seekers Search Jobs Job Seeker Login Advice & News Newsletter Sign-Up Employers Post A Job All Recruitment Products Employer Login Testimonials Company About Us Contact Us Advertise with Us Our Audience Privacy Policy | Terms of Use | Do Not Sell or Share My Personal Information © 2026 HigherEdJo